RFC-0000 Introduction
Abstract

This document defines the fundamental principles governing every compliant Memory Operating System (MOS).

These principles establish the canonical representation of cognition, define the universal cognitive primitives, specify invariant behaviors, and describe the semantics that all compliant implementations SHALL preserve.

No implementation detail—including databases, graph engines, APIs, programming languages, embedding models, or language models—may violate these principles.

1. Purpose

The purpose of this document is to establish the immutable foundations upon which the Memory Operating System is built.

These principles define what cognition means inside MOS.

Future specifications MAY extend the architecture but SHALL NOT contradict the principles defined in this document.

2. Scope

This RFC specifies:

The Cognitive Kernel
The Directed Cognitive Graph
Cognitive Elements
Identity
Provenance
Versioning
Confidence
Cognitive State
Cognitive Views
Cognitive Dynamics

This RFC intentionally does not specify:

Storage
APIs
Network protocols
SQL schemas
Serialization
Worker implementations

Those belong to companion specifications.

3. The Cognitive Kernel

MOS defines a minimal cognitive kernel.

Every compliant implementation SHALL preserve this model.

The kernel consists of exactly four concepts.

Cognitive State Space

↓

Cognitive Elements

↓

Cognitive Dynamics

↓

Cognitive Views

Everything else within MOS is derived from these four concepts.

4. The Cognitive Axiom

MOS defines one canonical representation of cognition.

A Memory Operating System SHALL represent cognition as a Cognitive State Space composed of versioned Cognitive Elements connected by typed Cognitive Relationships and exposed through one or more Cognitive Views.

This statement is the primary axiom of MOS.

All subsequent specifications derive from it.

5. Normative Axioms

The following axioms SHALL hold for every compliant implementation.

Axiom 1

Everything is a Cognitive Element.

No persistent cognitive information SHALL exist outside a Cognitive Element.

Axiom 2

Every Cognitive Element exists within the Cognitive State Space.

No Cognitive Element SHALL exist independently.

Axiom 3

Every Cognitive Element possesses identity.

Identity SHALL remain stable throughout its lifetime.

Axiom 4

Every Cognitive Element possesses provenance.

Its origin SHALL always be recoverable.

Axiom 5

Every Cognitive Element is versioned.

Historical versions SHALL remain accessible.

Axiom 6

Every Cognitive Element has temporal validity.

Time SHALL be considered part of cognition.

Axiom 7

Every Cognitive Element may participate in multiple relationships.

Relationships SHALL NOT be limited by object type.

Axiom 8

Every Cognitive Element is explainable.

Its existence SHALL be justifiable using stored cognitive state.

Axiom 9

Knowledge SHALL emerge from evidence.

Knowledge SHALL NOT appear without supporting evidence.

Axiom 10

Beliefs MAY conflict.

MOS SHALL permit simultaneous competing beliefs.

Axiom 11

Confidence is independent of truth.

Confidence SHALL express certainty rather than correctness.

Axiom 12

Cognition is continuously evolving.

No cognitive state SHALL be considered permanently final.

6. Cognitive State Space

MOS represents cognition as a continuously evolving state space.

The Cognitive State Space contains:

Cognitive Elements
Relationships
Versions
Temporal states
Provenance chains
Views

The State Space is the canonical representation.

Graphs, tables, indexes, caches, and prompts are derived representations.

7. Cognitive Elements

A Cognitive Element is the universal primitive of MOS.

Every persistent cognitive artifact SHALL be represented as a Cognitive Element.

Two categories exist.
Cognitive Element

├── Cognitive Object

└── Cognitive Relationship

No other primitive types exist.

8. Cognitive Objects

Cognitive Objects represent persistent cognitive artifacts.

Examples include:

Observation
Entity
Evidence
Claim
Belief
Knowledge
Goal
Plan
Task
Procedure
Skill
Prediction
Reflection
Evaluation
Action
Execution
Document
Artifact
Preference
Policy

Future specifications MAY define additional object types.

9. Cognitive Relationships

Relationships are Cognitive Elements.

Relationships SHALL possess:

Identity
Version
Provenance
Confidence
Metadata
Temporal validity

Relationships MAY themselves participate in additional relationships.

Example:

Evidence

↓

supports

↓

Claim

↓

strengthened_by

↓

Observation

MOS therefore permits reasoning about relationships themselves.

10. Cognitive Identity

Identity is conceptual rather than implementation-specific.

Each Cognitive Element SHALL expose:

Stable Identity
Current Version
Historical Versions
Lineage
Aliases

Identifiers such as UUIDs are implementation choices.

Identity semantics remain implementation independent.

11. Provenance

Every Cognitive Element SHALL preserve complete provenance.

Minimum provenance includes:

Creator
Source
Timestamp
Parent
Derivation
Producing worker
Producing model
Supporting evidence

The provenance chain SHALL be reconstructable.

12. Versioning

Every mutable concept SHALL evolve through immutable versions.

Previous versions SHALL remain accessible.

Version lineage SHALL be explicit.

Version graphs MAY branch.

MOS therefore supports alternative histories when required.

13. Confidence

Confidence represents epistemic certainty.

Confidence SHALL NOT represent truth.

Confidence MAY evolve.

Confidence SHALL remain historically traceable.

Confidence SHALL preserve its derivation.

14. Cognitive State

Every Cognitive Element occupies a cognitive state.

Recommended states include:

Created

Observed

Validated

Linked

Referenced

Strengthened

Weakened

Superseded

Archived

Implementations MAY define additional states provided semantic compatibility is maintained.

15. Cognitive Dynamics

MOS defines cognition as state transitions.

Transitions SHALL preserve:

Identity
Provenance
Version history

Transitions SHALL never destroy historical cognition.

16. Cognitive Views

Working Memory

Semantic Memory

Planning Memory

Reflection Memory

Learning Memory

Execution Memory

Archive Memory

are not separate storage systems.

They are projections of the Cognitive State Space.

Views SHALL NOT duplicate canonical cognition.

Views MAY materialize optimized representations.

17. Directed Cognitive Graph

The Directed Cognitive Graph (DCG) is the canonical graph projection of the Cognitive State Space.

The DCG consists of:

Cognitive Elements
Typed Cognitive Relationships
Version chains
Provenance links
Temporal links

The DCG is authoritative for traversal semantics.

Alternative storage implementations SHALL preserve equivalent graph semantics.

18. Attention

MOS introduces Attention as a first-class cognitive mechanism.

Attention determines which Cognitive Elements receive computational focus.

Attention influences:

Working Memory
Retrieval
Planning
Reflection
Consolidation
Memory decay

Attention SHALL be represented explicitly rather than implicitly.

Future specifications define Attention semantics.

19. Explainability

Every Cognitive Element SHALL support explanation.

An implementation SHALL reconstruct:

Supporting observations
Evidence
Claims
Beliefs
Reasoning
Decisions
Relationships

Explanation SHALL NOT require regenerting reasoning using external model.

20. Separation of Concerns

Observation

Memory

Reasoning

Planning

Execution

Reflection

Learning

Retrieval

Storage

are distinct cognitive responsibilities.

No component SHALL assume responsibilities belonging to another.

21. Derived Principles

The following principles emerge naturally from the axioms.

Memory is append-only.
Historical knowledge is never destroyed.
Knowledge evolves continuously.
Multiple perspectives may coexist.
Every conclusion is traceable.
Every belief is revisable.
Retrieval operates over views rather than raw storage.
Reasoning is reproducible.
Reflection modifies cognition rather than history.
Learning modifies future behavior without erasing the past.

These principles are consequences of the axioms rather than independent requirements.

22. Compliance

An implementation MAY claim:

MOS Core v0.1 Compliant

only if every normative requirement contained within this RFC is satisfied.

